=== 3. c = 2 is the boundary: the distance goes to zero, and how fast ===
        k      dist to 1/2       k * dist
       24     0.0647024909       1.552860
       40     0.0389170165       1.556681
       56     0.0278506532       1.559637
       72     0.0216894306       1.561639
       88     0.0177619471       1.563051
   compare rem:leeyanglacunary: Im q_1 = 3*pi/(2k), so k*dist -> 3*pi/2 = 4.712389

=== 4. the named profiles, against the same formula ===
        profile      k   1/R measured      predicted measured dist
           odds     70       0.500000       0.500000     0.503895
         primes     70       0.520310       0.460965     0.496799
      geometric     70       0.990005       0.005048     0.023069
   1/R read off a finite tail converges slowly from above, so the predicted column is
   an underestimate at these k; the odd numbers and the primes both have R = 2 exactly

=== 5. what the rewriting proves, and what it does not ===
   PROVED  : Gamma^(q) = 1 + G(2q) + G(2-2q) identically, so the limit is analytic exactly
             on |q| < R/2 and |1-q| < R/2, and the fair coin is interior iff R > 1.
             R >= 1 is equivalent to Gamma(A) < infinity, since G(1) = (Gamma-1)/2.
   MEASURED: dist(1/2, nearest zero) -> 1/c - 1/2 on the interpolating family.
   OPEN    : that the zeros accumulate ON the boundary rather than merely not inside it.
             Hurwitz gives one direction on compact subsets of the analytic region; the
             other needs the limit to be non-vanishing there, which for these profiles is
             a statement about G and is not proved here.
